bytes and molecules every living cell  has its own hard drive where it stores  bytes of information needed to function  and grow adapt to environmental changes  and fight off dangerous intruders this  information is stored in molecules that  are arranged in sequences of letters  referred to as DNA code in the 1990s a  tremendous effort by governments  researchers and companies resulted in  the full sequence of the human genome  since then we've seen an exponential  growth of molecular data that describes  cells tissue organs and organisms  massive datasets containing genetic  information about the young old healthy  and sick are now freely available to the  public here we will explore the various  omics data types as well as examples of  the information they contain in genomic  variants associated with ancestry gene  expression levels responsible for cancer  spread and protein modifications that  can render important code unreadable  this course is intended for all those  interested in understanding how the  current revolution in biomedical data  impacts biomedical research and human  health  [Music]  bytes and molecules
 

